WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.786
APIs are the interfaces that let
applications, services, users, devices,

2
00:00:05.866 --> 00:00:08.383
and data systems talk to each other.

3
00:00:08.463 --> 00:00:12.323
A public mobile app may call an API to
read account data.

4
00:00:12.403 --> 00:00:15.507
A browser may call an API to update a
profile.

5
00:00:15.587 --> 00:00:20.789
Internal services may call APIs to process
orders, synchronize records,

6
00:00:20.869 --> 00:00:22.463
or trigger workflows.

7
00:00:22.543 --> 00:00:24.725
REST, GraphQL, RPC, webhooks,

8
00:00:24.805 --> 00:00:28.580
and internal service APIs all have
different styles,

9
00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:32.017
but they share the same basic security
concern:

10
00:00:32.097 --> 00:00:35.872
they expose behavior that other software
can invoke.

11
00:00:35.952 --> 00:00:41.490
APIs are often security-critical because
they sit close to business logic and

12
00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:42.745
sensitive data.

13
00:00:42.825 --> 00:00:46.516
A user interface may hide an
administrative button,

14
00:00:46.596 --> 00:00:49.869
but the API behind that button may still
exist.

15
00:00:49.949 --> 00:00:53.305
A mobile app may show only one tenant's
records,

16
00:00:53.385 --> 00:00:57.160
but the API may accept an object
identifier directly.

17
00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:01.100
A partner integration may be trusted for
one workflow,

18
00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:06.382
but the endpoint may still process inputs
that affect billing, identity,

19
00:01:06.462 --> 00:01:08.560
inventory, or customer data.

20
00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:11.996
API security is not just web security with
JSON.

21
00:01:12.076 --> 00:01:16.942
Web security concepts still matter, but
APIs add their own patterns:

22
00:01:17.022 --> 00:01:21.301
machine identities, tokens, object-level
authorization,

23
00:01:21.381 --> 00:01:26.331
schema contracts, service-to-service
calls, pagination controls,

24
00:01:26.411 --> 00:01:31.949
data shaping, replay considerations, and
high-volume automation pressure.

25
00:01:32.029 --> 00:01:35.385
Many API failures are not about rendering
HTML.

26
00:01:35.465 --> 00:01:41.087
They are about exposing an operation too
broadly or accepting a request that the

27
00:01:41.167 --> 00:01:43.600
business never intended to allow.

28
00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:48.295
A strong API security program combines
design, implementation,

29
00:01:48.375 --> 00:01:51.647
configuration, monitoring, and operations.

30
00:01:51.727 --> 00:01:55.838
Endpoints should have explicit owners,
clear contracts,

31
00:01:55.918 --> 00:02:01.624
least-privileged access rules, validated
inputs, careful response behavior,

32
00:02:01.704 --> 00:02:04.892
abuse controls, and production visibility.

33
00:02:04.972 --> 00:02:07.993
The goal is not to make APIs harder to
build.

34
00:02:08.073 --> 00:02:13.359
The goal is to make every exposed
interface intentional, understandable,

35
00:02:13.439 --> 00:02:15.620
and resilient against misuse.
